# API conventions

## Layout

```
backend/routes/api.php                          # /api/v1/...
backend/app/Http/Controllers/Api/ApiBaseController.php
backend/app/Http/Controllers/Api/V1/{Domain}/   # invokable, final (one action each)
backend/app/Http/Requests/Api/V1/{Domain}/
backend/app/Http/Resources/Api/V1/
backend/app/Policies/                           # when authorization is needed
backend/app/Services/{Domain}/                  # only when business logic warrants it
backend/app/DTOs/{Domain}/                      # *Result DTOs for those services
backend/tests/Feature/Api/V1/{Domain}/
```

New endpoints: `/api/v1/...`, named `api.v1.{domain}.{action}`. Health stays `/api/v1/health`.

## Controllers

Always use **invokable** single-action controllers (`final`, `__invoke` only). Do **not** use multi-method resource controllers.

For simple CRUD and straightforward auth (login/logout/me), call Auth/Eloquent (and authorize via Policy when needed) inside the controller—no Service layer required:

```php
final class StoreUserController extends ApiBaseController
{
    public function __invoke(StoreUserRequest $request): JsonResponse
    {
        $user = User::query()->create([/* ... */]);

        return $this->respondCreated([
            'message' => 'User created.',
            'data' => ['user' => UserResource::make($user)],
        ]);
    }
}
```

When an action has non-trivial business logic, method-inject a service (see service-result-dto rule). Method-inject form requests; no constructor DI.

- Use `respondOk` / `respondCreated` / `respondUnauthorized` / `respondForbidden` / `respondNotFound` / `respondNoContent`
- Auth: `auth:sanctum` for protected routes

## Tests

Pest. `RefreshDatabase`. Hit `/api/v1/...` with `postJson` / `getJson`. Mirror `tests/Feature/Api/V1/Auth/`.
